    pros: [
      "Uses your rack, so it adds almost nothing to your footprint",
      "Quick-mount J-hook with a nylon liner to protect the uprights",
      "Seat adjusts with a knob between seated extensions and standing curls",
      "The cheapest dedicated quad and hamstring option on this page",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Fits metric 3x3 uprights only; Titan says it will not fit imperial 3x3",
      "A 265 lb user limit and 275 lb plate capacity",
      "Standing curl only, and no adjustable seat depth to line your knee up",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The space-saver for owners of a TITAN Series or other metric rack. Check your uprights first; Titan sells a different version for T-3 and X-3 racks.",
    description: "This attachment hooks inside a power rack and gives you a padded seat for leg extensions and a standing position for leg curls, loaded with Olympic plates on a 50 mm sleeve. Titan lists it at 43 inches wide, 25 inches deep and 18 inches tall, 76 lb, with a 265 lb maximum user weight and a 275 lb plate capacity, and BarBend gives the same dimensions and capacities. Where they differ is fit: Titan says it fits TITAN Series racks and metric 3 by 3 inch uprights — 2.95 inches square with 26 mm holes on 50 mm spacing — and will not fit imperial 3 by 3 uprights, while BarBend describes it as fitting any 3 by 3 rack with one-inch holes. Titan's own Amazon listing, which appeared in Amazon's search results on 19 October 2026, repeats the metric-only warning, so go by Titan. BarBend's reservations are the standing curl, the lack of an adjustable seat pad and the 265 lb user limit. It was $449.97, down from $599.99, on Titan's store on 19 October 2026, with a one-year warranty; Titan sells a separate rack-mounted leg curl and extension for its T-3 and X-3 racks at $419.97.",
